body:
An approval card should change after someone decides, otherwise a second approver can click again. Universal Actions (Action.Execute, schema 1.4 and later) send a verb and the inputs to your bot or Workflows endpoint, which replies with a replacement card. The card below is plain schema 1.5 JSON.

## Card JSON

```json
{
  "type": "AdaptiveCard",
  "$schema": "http://adaptivecards.io/schemas/adaptive-card.json",
  "version": "1.5",
  "msteams": {
    "width": "Full"
  },
  "refresh": {
    "action": {
      "type": "Action.Execute",
      "verb": "refreshApproval",
      "title": "Refresh",
      "data": {
        "requestId": "REQ-1042"
      }
    },
    "userIds": [
      "8:orgid:11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555"
    ]
  },
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "Purchase request REQ-1042",
      "wrap": true,
      "size": "Large",
      "weight": "Bolder"
    },
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "Waiting for your decision",
      "wrap": true,
      "isSubtle": true,
      "spacing": "None"
    },
    {
      "type": "FactSet",
      "facts": [
        {
          "title": "Requester",
          "value": "Alex Contoso"
        },
        {
          "title": "Item",
          "value": "Surface Laptop 5, 2 units"
        },
        {
          "title": "Amount",
          "value": "EUR 3,980.00"
        },
        {
          "title": "Cost center",
          "value": "CC-2210"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Text",
      "id": "comment",
      "label": "Comment",
      "placeholder": "Add a reason (required when rejecting)",
      "isMultiline": true,
      "maxLength": 500
    }
  ],
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "Action.Execute",
      "title": "Approve",
      "verb": "approve",
      "style": "positive",
      "data": {
        "requestId": "REQ-1042",
        "decision": "approved"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "Action.Execute",
      "title": "Reject",
      "verb": "reject",
      "style": "destructive",
      "data": {
        "requestId": "REQ-1042",
        "decision": "rejected"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "Action.OpenUrl",
      "title": "Open request",
      "url": "https://contoso.example/requests/REQ-1042"
    }
  ]
}
```

## How to use

1. Send the card as an attachment with contentType application/vnd.microsoft.card.adaptive from a Teams bot, or paste it into the Card Designer to test the layout.
2. Handle the invoke activity adaptiveCard/action. Read the verb (approve, reject, refreshApproval), the data object and the comment from activity.value.action.data. The bot answers with HTTP 200 and a card (application/vnd.microsoft.card.adaptive) that no longer has the buttons, for example "Approved by Sam at 14:05".
3. The refresh block with userIds asks Teams to call your bot with verb refreshApproval when a listed user views the card, so each approver can see a personalized or up-to-date version. Replace the placeholder user id with the Teams or Microsoft Entra id format your bot receives.
4. When the comment is required for a rejection, check it on the server and answer with the same card plus a red TextBlock that says what is missing. Do not set isRequired on the input, because it would block Approve too.

## Common mistakes

- Using Action.Submit and expecting the card to update. Action.Submit sends data as a message; only Action.Execute lets the bot return a new card for the same message.
- Mixing Action.Submit and Action.Execute in one card. Teams treats a card as one or the other; pick Action.Execute for the whole card.
- Forgetting that style positive and destructive are hints. Teams may ignore them; do not rely on color alone for meaning, the button titles say it already.
- Putting the amount in the card without validation on the server. The client can only send what it was given; look up the request id on the server and ignore any amount in the posted data.
- Omitting requestId from the data. Without it a bot cannot tell which request the click refers to.

## Compatibility notes

- Action.Execute needs schema 1.4 or higher. The card declares 1.5, which Teams desktop, web and mobile render.
- The msteams width Full property makes the card use the full message width in Teams.
- In Outlook, Action.Execute works only for registered Universal Action providers; a Teams card posted to a channel does not work in Outlook as is.
- Keep the card payload under the Teams size limit for a message (about 28 KB for the whole activity).

Most useful bot cards are forms. Since schema 1.3 inputs support label, isRequired and errorMessage, so the client does validation before anything is sent. This card collects a helpdesk ticket and shows each input type.

## Card JSON

```json
{
  "type": "AdaptiveCard",
  "$schema": "http://adaptivecards.io/schemas/adaptive-card.json",
  "version": "1.5",
  "msteams": {
    "width": "Full"
  },
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "Report an IT problem",
      "wrap": true,
      "size": "Large",
      "weight": "Bolder"
    },
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "Fields marked with * are required.",
      "wrap": true,
      "isSubtle": true,
      "spacing": "None"
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Text",
      "id": "title",
      "label": "Short summary *",
      "isRequired": true,
      "errorMessage": "Please enter a summary",
      "maxLength": 120,
      "placeholder": "For example: VPN drops every 10 minutes"
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.ChoiceSet",
      "id": "category",
      "label": "Category *",
      "isRequired": true,
      "errorMessage": "Pick a category",
      "style": "filtered",
      "placeholder": "Type to search",
      "choices": [
        {
          "title": "Network and VPN",
          "value": "network"
        },
        {
          "title": "Email and calendar",
          "value": "mail"
        },
        {
          "title": "Hardware",
          "value": "hardware"
        },
        {
          "title": "Software install",
          "value": "software"
        },
        {
          "title": "Access and permissions",
          "value": "access"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.ChoiceSet",
      "id": "impact",
      "label": "Who is affected? *",
      "isRequired": true,
      "errorMessage": "Select one option",
      "style": "expanded",
      "value": "me",
      "choices": [
        {
          "title": "Only me",
          "value": "me"
        },
        {
          "title": "My team",
          "value": "team"
        },
        {
          "title": "Everyone in the office",
          "value": "office"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Date",
      "id": "firstSeen",
      "label": "When did it start?",
      "max": "2030-12-31",
      "min": "2024-01-01"
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Number",
      "id": "devices",
      "label": "Number of affected devices",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 50,
      "value": 1
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Text",
      "id": "email",
      "label": "Contact email *",
      "style": "email",
      "isRequired": true,
      "regex": "^[^@\\s]+@[^@\\s]+\\.[^@\\s]+$",
      "errorMessage": "Enter a valid email address",
      "placeholder": "name@contoso.example"
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Text",
      "id": "details",
      "label": "Details",
      "isMultiline": true,
      "maxLength": 1000
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Toggle",
      "id": "callBack",
      "title": "Please call me back",
      "valueOn": "yes",
      "valueOff": "no",
      "value": "no"
    }
  ],
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "Action.Submit",
      "title": "Submit ticket",
      "data": {
        "intent": "createTicket"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "Action.Submit",
      "title": "Cancel",
      "associatedInputs": "none",
      "data": {
        "intent": "cancelTicket"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## How to use

1. Send the card from a Teams bot, a Workflows "Post card in a chat or channel" action or a Copilot Studio Adaptive Card node.
2. Read the posted values by input id: title, category, impact, firstSeen, devices, email, details, callBack. The Submit action also posts the data object (intent: createTicket), which tells your handler which button was used.
3. Validate again on the server. Client checks are convenience, not trust.
4. The Cancel button uses associatedInputs none, so it does not run validation or send the inputs. Without that, a user could not cancel with an empty required field.

## Common mistakes

- Marking inputs required but giving no errorMessage. The user then sees a generic message or none at all.
- Using style filtered on a client that only supports 1.4 or lower; it falls back to compact, which is acceptable here. Keep the choice list short (below 50 items) for this reason.
- Putting a regex in the card without escaping backslashes in JSON. The pattern ^[^@\\s]+@... must have double backslashes in the file.
- Expecting Input.Date to return a time. It returns yyyy-MM-dd; use Input.Time or a second field if you need the time (Input.Time is not shown in Outlook).
- Giving two inputs the same id. The second silently overwrites the first in the posted data.

## Compatibility notes

- label, isRequired and errorMessage need version 1.3; the filtered ChoiceSet style needs 1.5; associatedInputs on Action.Submit needs 1.3.
- Teams supports Action.Submit; in Teams the posted values arrive in activity.value of the message back to your bot.
- Outlook actionable messages do not support Action.Submit; use Action.Http there (see the Outlook entry of this library).
- Do not use secrets or personal data defaults in the card; the JSON is visible to the client.

Before schema 1.5 the only way to show columns of numbers was a ColumnSet per row, which broke alignment on narrow screens. The Table element gives real rows and columns, header handling and grid lines. This card renders an order summary and degrades gracefully.

## Card JSON

