## How to use

1. Generate the rows from your data: one TableRow per line item, each with the same number of TableCell objects as there are columns.
2. columns defines widths. Numbers 3, 1, 1 are relative weights; you can also use pixel widths such as "80px".
3. firstRowAsHeaders marks the first row as header (accessible to screen readers). Style the header row with style accent and the total row with emphasis.
4. Use horizontalAlignment Right inside the cell text for numbers so decimals line up.
5. The fallback on the Table is rendered by clients that do not know the element (older Outlook or mobile builds). Keep it short and in schema 1.0 elements only.

## Common mistakes

- Rows with a different number of cells than columns. Missing cells shift the layout; add an empty cell as in the total row.
- Setting the card version below 1.5. A Table in a 1.4 card is an unknown element and triggers the fallback or is dropped.
- Wide tables. Teams mobile and side panels are narrow; keep to three or four columns and use short headers.
- Putting long free text in cells. Use a ColumnSet or a Container with wrapped TextBlocks instead.
- Using Table cells for layout of buttons. Cells hold content, not actions; use an ActionSet outside the table.

## Compatibility notes

- Table needs schema 1.5. Teams supports it on desktop, web and mobile current builds; Outlook support depends on client version, so rely on the fallback.
- Properties used here (gridStyle, firstRowAsHeaders, showGridLines, style on TableRow) all exist in the 1.5 schema.
- Teams ignores style positive and destructive, but container and row styles such as accent and emphasis render as theme-aware backgrounds in light, dark and contrast modes.
- For a templated version with a variable number of rows, use $data on the TableRow (see the templating entry).

Polls are the easiest way to get people to use a bot. This card shows the three ChoiceSet styles and a toggle in one Submit. The multi-select answer arrives as a comma-separated string.

## Card JSON

```json
{
  "type": "AdaptiveCard",
  "$schema": "http://adaptivecards.io/schemas/adaptive-card.json",
  "version": "1.5",
  "msteams": {
    "width": "Full"
  },
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "Team offsite: pick your options",
      "wrap": true,
      "size": "Large",
      "weight": "Bolder"
    },
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "Closes Friday 17:00. You can change your answer until then.",
      "wrap": true,
      "isSubtle": true,
      "spacing": "None"
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.ChoiceSet",
      "id": "date",
      "label": "Preferred date (choose one)",
      "style": "expanded",
      "isRequired": true,
      "errorMessage": "Choose a date",
      "choices": [
        {
          "title": "Tue 10 March",
          "value": "2026-03-10"
        },
        {
          "title": "Wed 11 March",
          "value": "2026-03-11"
        },
        {
          "title": "Thu 12 March",
          "value": "2026-03-12"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.ChoiceSet",
      "id": "activities",
      "label": "Activities you would join (choose any)",
      "style": "expanded",
      "isMultiSelect": true,
      "value": "workshop",
      "choices": [
        {
          "title": "Workshop",
          "value": "workshop"
        },
        {
          "title": "Cooking class",
          "value": "cooking"
        },
        {
          "title": "Hike",
          "value": "hike"
        },
        {
          "title": "Escape room",
          "value": "escape"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.ChoiceSet",
      "id": "diet",
      "label": "Dietary needs",
      "style": "compact",
      "placeholder": "None",
      "choices": [
        {
          "title": "Vegetarian",
          "value": "veg"
        },
        {
          "title": "Vegan",
          "value": "vegan"
        },
        {
          "title": "Gluten free",
          "value": "gf"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Toggle",
      "id": "plusOne",
      "title": "I will bring a guest",
      "valueOn": "true",
      "valueOff": "false",
      "value": "false"
    }
  ],
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "Action.Submit",
      "title": "Send my answer",
      "data": {
        "pollId": "offsite-2026",
        "msteams": {
          "type": "messageBack",
          "displayText": "Answer sent",
          "text": "poll-answer"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## How to use

1. Post the card to a channel or chat through your bot. Keep the pollId in the Submit data so that one handler can serve many polls.
2. The bot receives something like {"pollId":"offsite-2026","date":"2026-03-11","activities":"workshop,hike","diet":"veg","plusOne":"false"}. Split activities on the comma.
3. Store the answer under pollId plus the sender's user id (Teams id or Microsoft Entra id) so a second submit replaces the first. This gives you "change your answer until the deadline" for free.
4. After the deadline respond with a summary card, for example a Table of counts.
5. The msteams messageBack block sets the text shown in the chat (displayText) and the text sent to the bot (text). Use it when you want a visible confirmation line in the conversation.

## Common mistakes

- Reading a multi-select as an array. It is a single string of values joined by commas; values themselves must not contain commas.
- Not setting an initial value for a toggle. Without value, the posted data may omit it or send valueOff depending on the client.
- Using style expanded with twenty options. The card becomes long; use compact or filtered for long lists.
- Assuming the card is anonymous. Teams tells the bot who submitted, so say in the card text if answers are visible to the organizer.
- Letting people vote after the deadline. Check the time on the server when the Submit arrives and reply with an updated card without buttons.

## Compatibility notes

- isMultiSelect, style compact and expanded exist since 1.0; label, isRequired and errorMessage need 1.3.
- messageBack in the data object is a Teams extension; other hosts ignore it.
- In Copilot Studio use the Ask with Adaptive Card node and map each input id to a variable; the multi-select value is again a delimited string.
- Cards sent through incoming webhooks cannot use Action.Submit; use a bot or Workflows with Action.Execute or Action.OpenUrl instead.

Hard-coding cards in strings does not scale. Adaptive Card Templating separates layout (a template with ${binding} expressions) from data (any JSON). The template below renders a variable-length task list, highlights overdue items and hides empty notes.

## Card JSON

```json
{
  "type": "AdaptiveCard",
  "$schema": "http://adaptivecards.io/schemas/adaptive-card.json",
  "version": "1.5",
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "${title}",
      "wrap": true,
      "size": "Large",
      "weight": "Bolder"
    },
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "${count(tasks)} open tasks, ${count(where(tasks, t, t.overdue))} overdue",
      "wrap": true,
      "isSubtle": true,
      "spacing": "None"
    },
    {
      "type": "Container",
      "$data": "${tasks}",
      "separator": true,
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "ColumnSet",
          "columns": [
            {
              "type": "Column",
              "width": "stretch",
              "items": [
                {
                  "type": "TextBlock",
                  "text": "${name}",
                  "wrap": true,
                  "weight": "Bolder"
                },
                {
                  "type": "TextBlock",
                  "text": "${owner} - due ${formatDateTime(due, 'MMM d')}",
                  "wrap": true,
                  "isSubtle": true,
                  "spacing": "None"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Column",
              "width": "auto",
              "items": [
                {
                  "type": "TextBlock",
                  "text": "${priority}",
                  "wrap": true,
                  "color": "${if(overdue, 'Attention', 'Default')}",
                  "weight": "Bolder"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "TextBlock",
          "$when": "${notes != ''}",
          "text": "${notes}",
          "wrap": true,
          "size": "Small",
          "isSubtle": true
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "Action.OpenUrl",
      "title": "Open board",
      "url": "${boardUrl}"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Sample data

```json
{
  "title": "Sprint 14 board",
  "boardUrl": "https://contoso.example/boards/sprint-14",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "name": "Fix login timeout",
      "owner": "Alex",
      "due": "2026-03-05T00:00:00Z",
      "priority": "High",
      "overdue": true,
      "notes": "Blocks release candidate"
    },
    {
      "name": "Update onboarding guide",
      "owner": "Sam",
      "due": "2026-03-12T00:00:00Z",
      "priority": "Low",
      "overdue": false,
      "notes": ""
    }
  ]
}
```

## Expand the template

JavaScript (npm package adaptivecards-templating):

```javascript
import * as ACData from "adaptivecards-templating";
const template = new ACData.Template(templateJson);
const card = template.expand({ $root: dataJson });
// send "card" as the attachment content
```

.NET (NuGet package AdaptiveCards.Templating):

```csharp
var template = new AdaptiveCardTemplate(templateJson);
string cardJson = template.Expand(dataJson);
```

## How to use

1. Design the layout in the Card Designer: open the sample data editor, paste the data above and the preview updates.
2. $data on the Container repeats it once for each element of tasks; inside it, names such as ${name} refer to the current task, while ${title} on the root needs $root.title when used inside the loop.
3. $when removes the notes TextBlock when the expression is false, so empty text does not leave a gap.
4. Expand on the server and send the resulting plain JSON. The client never sees the template syntax.

## Common mistakes

- Sending the unexpanded template to Teams. The placeholders show up as literal text.
- Forgetting $root inside a loop when you need a root value, for example ${$root.boardUrl}.
- Using a string for the boolean overdue ("false"). if("false", ...) is truthy; send real JSON booleans.
- Date formats: formatDateTime needs an ISO 8601 string. Pass 2026-03-05T00:00:00Z, not 05.03.2026.
- Concatenating user text into the template string. Put user text in the data object, which the engine escapes properly.

## Compatibility notes

- The template syntax is Adaptive Expressions; functions used here (if, count, where, formatDateTime) are part of it.
- The expanded card is schema 1.5 and uses only TextBlock, Container, ColumnSet and Action.OpenUrl, so it renders on Teams, Outlook (version dependent) and Copilot Studio.
- Power Automate actions that post an adaptive card do not expand templates; use a Compose action with the expanded text or expand in code first.

Outlook handles Adaptive Cards differently from Teams: it uses the actionable message format, wants an originator id and calls your service with Action.Http, not Action.Submit. This card lets a manager approve an expense directly in the mail, with an optional comment.

## Card JSON

```json
{
