## How to use

1. Register a provider in the Actionable Email Developer Dashboard (Microsoft documents it for Outlook and Exchange). It gives you an originator GUID; replace the zero GUID in the card. Scope it to your sender address and service domain.
2. Embed the card in the message HTML inside a script tag of type application/adaptivecard+json in the head, together with a normal HTML body as fallback for clients that cannot render cards. You can send it with Microsoft Graph sendMail, SMTP or the Power Automate action Send an email.
3. Your endpoint receives a POST with the substituted body. It validates the bearer token that Outlook adds in the Authorization header, and replies HTTP 200 on success. On failure, return 4xx with the CARD-ACTION-STATUS header; Outlook shows that text to the user.
4. To replace the card after the action, return the new card in the response body and add the header CARD-UPDATE-IN-BODY set to true.
5. Action.ToggleVisibility shows the comment input; the target element has isVisible false at the start.

## Common mistakes

- Using Action.Submit. Outlook does not display it. Use Action.Http, Action.OpenUrl, Action.ToggleVisibility or Action.ShowCard.
- Pointing the url at localhost or an internal name. Outlook calls from the Microsoft cloud, so the endpoint must be reachable on the internet and use HTTPS.
- Hard-coding secrets in the card. The mail is readable by the recipient; authenticate through the bearer token that Outlook sends and check it on the server.
- Sending to users outside your tenant without a registered and approved provider; the card then renders as plain HTML or not at all.
- Leaving the HTML body empty. Clients without card support show nothing useful.

## Compatibility notes

- Outlook actionable messages are documented with schema version 1.0; keep to TextBlock, Image, Container, ColumnSet, FactSet, Input.Text and ActionSet. Input.Time is not displayed.
- originator and hideOriginalBody are Outlook-specific root properties; Teams ignores them.
- The {{comment.value}} input substitution syntax works inside Action.Http url, body and header values.
- Universal Actions (Action.Execute) in Outlook need their own registration and a newer schema; check current Microsoft documentation if you want one card for Teams and Outlook.

Channel posts get ignored when they are long. Progressive disclosure keeps the default view to two lines and lets people open details or an inline form only when they need it. Two actions do the work: Action.ToggleVisibility flips existing elements, Action.ShowCard opens a nested card with its own inputs and buttons.

