## How to use

1. Give every element you want to toggle an id and isVisible false. targetElements takes element ids (strings) or objects with elementId and isVisible if you want to force a state rather than toggle.
2. The nested card in Action.ShowCard has no $schema or version of its own; it inherits from the parent. Its Submit posts reason, level and the data object.
3. Post the card from a bot. Toggling happens on the client and sends nothing to the server, so it works offline and costs nothing.
4. For a status that changes (Investigating to Resolved), update the card in place by replacing the activity with a newly generated card from your bot.

## Common mistakes

- Toggling an element without an id. The action silently does nothing.
- Putting required inputs in hidden elements of the main card. Hidden required inputs may block Submit validation in some clients; keep required inputs inside the ShowCard card, which is only validated when opened.
- Relying on ToggleVisibility for secrets. The hidden text is still part of the card JSON on the client.
- Many ShowCard actions. Teams displays them as an expandable section under the button row; more than two or three become confusing.
- Forgetting that screen readers need visible state: write the button title as "Show or hide details" (or change the card) so the action is understandable.

## Compatibility notes

- Action.ToggleVisibility and Action.ShowCard exist since schema 1.0 and render in Teams, Outlook and Copilot Studio (Outlook supports both in actionable messages).
- container style emphasis and the ColumnSet layout work from schema 1.0; color Warning and size Medium are standard TextBlock values.
- Action.Submit is not available in Outlook; swap in Action.Http there.
- Teams shows Action.ShowCard inline on desktop and web; on mobile it can open the nested card below the actions.

A status card should answer one question in two seconds: is anything broken? This layout uses a header band, one row per service and a color plus a text label for each state, so it stays readable for people who cannot distinguish colors.

