## How to use

1. Generate the rows from your monitoring data. Each row is a Container with a ColumnSet of three columns: dot, name and detail, state label.
2. Map your states to the TextBlock color names: Good (green), Warning (yellow), Attention (red), Accent, Dark, Light and Default. Do not use hex colors; Adaptive Cards do not support them and the named colors adapt to dark mode.
3. Post it to a channel on a schedule with a Workflows or Power Automate flow. A trigger on alert state changes keeps the channel quiet when all is well.
4. To update in place, keep the message id and replace the card each time; otherwise people scroll through a history of outdated boards.
5. Viva Connections dashboards can use the same JSON for an Adaptive Card Extension quick view with small adjustments.

## Common mistakes

- Communicating state only by color. The state label (Healthy, Degraded, Outage) is part of every row for this reason.
- Using images for status icons that load from external hosts. Blocked images leave empty gaps; the colored bullet is plain text.
- Forgetting wrap. Long detail lines are cut off in a narrow client; wrap true is set on every TextBlock.
- Putting 30 services into one card. Show only unhealthy ones plus a summary line, and link to the full status page.
- Stating "updated" times without timezone. The card uses UTC explicitly.

## Compatibility notes

- bleed on a Container and container style need 1.2; RichTextBlock and TextRun need 1.2. Everything else works from 1.0.
- msteams width Full lets the card use the whole message width; Outlook and Copilot Studio ignore the property.
- verticalContentAlignment on Column requires schema 1.1 or higher.
- A card should stay below roughly 28 KB in Teams; with this row template that is around 150 rows, far above what you should show.
